NETWORKING DAY 05 NOTES — nmap Part 2
Date: 03-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- -sS (SYN scan) needs root, never completes the full TCP handshake - stealthier, nmap's default as root
- -sT (Connect scan) completes the full handshake, works without root, more "visible" to logging
- -sU (UDP scan) is a different protocol entirely - slower, and results are often uncertain (open|filtered)
- Timing templates T0 (paranoid/slowest) through T5 (insane/fastest) trade speed for stealth/accuracy
- T3 is the default if you don't specify a timing template
- --top-ports N lets you pick exactly how many common ports to check, -F is shorthand for top 100
- A slower scan generates less traffic and fewer alerts - this is WHY real attackers sometimes go slow on purpose

COMMANDS YOU PRACTICED:
[ ] sudo nmap -sS <target>  vs  nmap -sT <target>
[ ] sudo nmap -sU -p <ports> <target>
[ ] nmap -T2 vs nmap -T4 (with 'time' to measure)
[ ] nmap -p <list>,<range> <target>
[ ] nmap --top-ports 20 <target>

MY OWN OUTPUT (paste your real output here):
$ sudo nmap -sS scanme.nmap.org
> 

$ sudo nmap -sU -p 53,67,123,161 scanme.nmap.org
> 

$ time nmap -T4 -F scanme.nmap.org
> 


